   "notes": [
    "Actual results from the 2024 Annual Audit and Financial Report filed with the PA Department of Community and Economic Development, not a budget.",
    "Philadelphia is a combined city and county, so these figures include county services. School-district taxes and independent authorities are separate.",
    "Left out: other financing sources ($1,842,750,855) and uses ($1,595,583,000), such as transfers between the municipality's own funds and borrowing, which the statewide report does not break down.",
    "\"Other expenditures\" ($1,600,763,000, mostly insurance, pensions and employee benefits) are spread across departments in proportion to their size.",
    "Political money is not scored: Pennsylvania municipal candidates file campaign reports with their county board of elections, and there is no statewide database of those filings.",
    "Transparency: City of Philadelphia has filed its annual audit and financial report with the Department of Community and Economic Development for FY 2024, the latest year most PA governments have filed. This is a check that the report was filed, not an opinion on its contents. The other five practices have not been checked yet, so they are left out of the score.",
    "Red flags: surveillance programs come from the EFF Atlas of Surveillance, a public database of news reports and public records, searched on 2026-10-15. A government with none listed may still have programs nobody has reported. Most records carry no dollar amount, so each documented program costs a quarter of the red-flag points instead of being counted as spending.",
    "Red flags: 122 license-plate cameras are mapped inside City of Philadelphia's borders in OpenStreetMap (44 made by Flock Safety), not counting cameras tagged as run by stores, malls, schools or state and regional agencies. Flock cameras count as a red flag whoever runs them. Other brands count only when the government is the recorded operator, since many have no operator recorded."
   ],
